    {:method "POST" :path "/api/v1/graphs/:graph-id/e2ee/encrypt" :internal-path "/e2ee/encrypt"
@@ -195,6 +200,21 @@
    "getAsset" ["Get an asset download link" "Returns asset metadata and a five-minute signed URL for the asset block's R2 object. The URL does not require an Authorization header. On plain graphs, use it to display image content. On E2EE graphs the object contains encrypted file bytes and must be decrypted with the graph AES key before display."]
    "searchGraph" ["Search graph resources" "Searches pages, blocks, tags, properties, and assets by title and returns cursor-paginated results. An exact title match ranks first, then pages, tags, properties and assets, then blocks."]
    "listBlockChanges" ["List changed blocks for a search index" "Feeds an external search index. Without since, lists every content block in UUID order. With since (the t of an earlier response) and that response's epoch, lists the blocks whose indexed text may have changed since then (their own text, an ancestor's or page's title, a referenced title, their tags, or their position) and, under removed, the UUIDs that are no longer content blocks. Each block carries its page title, its ancestors' text as a breadcrumb, its own text with references shown as titles, and the tags on it, its ancestors and its page. Answers 410 when the changes since that point are unavailable (the graph was re-uploaded or reset, or too much changed): list again without since. Unavailable for E2EE graphs."]
+   "datalogQuery" ["Run a read-only Datalog query"
+                   (str "Runs a read-only Datalog query against the graph and returns the rows. Prefer run_named_query when one of its queries fits (projects-untouched, meetings-without-decision, open-tasks-by-client, pages-missing-property): each reads a few datoms per row rather than scanning an attribute, except projects-untouched, which can reach the scan cap on a large graph (see run_named_query). query is the vector form [:find … :where …] or the map form {:find […] :where […]} as EDN, at most 4,000 characters, with the keys :find, :in, :where, :with and :keys (also :strs and :syms). inputs is an EDN vector of the values bound to :in after $ (at most 20 values and 4,000 characters; #uuid and keywords work), and rules an EDN vector of rules bound to % in :in (at most 4,000 characters)."
+                        " The query is read as data and never evaluated, and it runs on a read-only view of the graph: a write form (:db/add, :db/retract, :db/retractEntity, :db.fn/…, a map with :db/id) anywhere in query, inputs or rules is refused. Only pure functions can be called (comparisons, arithmetic, get-else, get-some, missing?, ground, untuple, count, clojure.string/includes?, starts-with?, ends-with?, lower-case, upper-case, blank? and the type predicates); rand, range, the regex functions, clojure.string/join, replace and split, the print functions, the collection constructors (vector, list, set, hash-map, array-map, tuple) and a function passed in through inputs (a ?variable in function position) are refused. str, + and keyword take at most one ?variable, the other arguments literals ([(str \"#\" ?t) ?s] runs, [(str ?a ?b) ?s] is refused), because a chain of clauses joining two values could double one at every clause. The only source is $, and % only with rules. Every where clause must share a variable with the others, through a data pattern, a function binding, a rule call, or, or-join, not or not-join: clauses linked only by a predicate are a cross product and are refused. A rule that calls itself, directly or through other rules, before a data pattern or a get-some binds one of the call's variables (left recursion, such as [(under ?b ?p) (under ?b ?x) (under ?x ?p)]) is refused too, because it expands without reading data and the scan cap can't stop it: put the data pattern first ([?b :block/parent ?x] (under ?x ?p)). A data pattern joined to two or more bound rows scans its whole attribute, so to walk up from bound blocks use get-some, which reads one datom per row ([(above ?b ?a) [(get-some $ ?b :block/parent) [_ ?x]] (above ?x ?a)]); get-else, ground and identity don't count as reading data, since they bind a value whether or not a datom exists."
+                        " Private content is left out, as if it weren't in the graph: anything tagged Private (any case) or with a tag that extends Private, every block on such a page or under such a block, and every reference to one of those, in rows, pulls (reverse refs too), missing?, get-else and aggregates. The Private tag itself stays visible. Content in the Recycle bin is left out the same way, as list_tasks and the other reads leave it out: a page delete_page moved there with its blocks, and a block delete_block moved there."
+                        " Work is capped, not timed: a query that scans more than 20,000 datoms (the scan cap; every datom it reads counts, private ones too) is stopped and refused with 422 naming the cap, never answered in part, so narrow it with a tag, page or attribute value. A query that matches more than 2,500 rows (the row cap) is refused with 422 naming the cap too, because every row is sorted before limit applies: a join can match far more rows than it reads datoms (a self-join through a tag on n blocks matches n × n), so bind one side, or count with an aggregate such as (count ?b), which folds the rows before the cap. A join is also bounded while Datascript builds it: a query that builds more than 20,000 rows while joining (the join cap; every pair a join through a shared value matches counts, in or-join, not-join and rule bodies too) is stopped with 422 naming the cap, and an aggregate doesn't help there, since it counts rows after the join. Rows whose collections (an inputs collection bound to a variable every row returns) hold more than 20,000 values in all (the value cap) are refused with 422 before the sort too; each character of text inside them counts as a value. Rows are sorted (entity ids, then values, by type), and cut to limit (1 to 200, default 50), then to about 100,000 characters of JSON (the byte cap), whole rows only; total-rows counts every row, and truncated says which cap cut them (by: limit or byte-cap) with a note. Scalar (?x .), collection ([?x ...]) and aggregate finds come back as rows and are cut the same way. Each (pull ?x pattern) in :find is pulled only for the rows kept, so a pull costs at most limit pulls: the pattern is a literal vector at most 2 levels deep ([:block/title {:block/parent [:block/title]}]), with no recursion (... or a recursion number) and no :xform, and a many attribute (and a reverse ref) returns at most 50 values; (limit :block/refs n) takes n from 1 to 50. Pull :block/uuid and :block/title to get handles for the other tools. Keywords come back as text without the colon (block/title), uuids as text and refs as numbers (entity ids)."
+                        " Each refusal is a 400 naming the clause (where 2, rules: rule name clause 1, find, find: pull ?b, :in) and the reason (a pull refusal names the attribute or the depth); a malformed query names the line and column, and an inputs count that doesn't match :in names both counts. With dry-run=true the query, inputs and rules come back as EDN without running. Unavailable for E2EE graphs (409): titles are ciphertext.")]
+   "runNamedQuery" ["Run a named read-only query"
+                    (str "Runs one of four vetted read-only queries by name, with params resolved against the graph, and returns rows as datalog_query does. name is one of:"
+                         " projects-untouched (params tag, default Project, and days, default 30): pages tagged tag, or a tag extending it, whose own updated-at is older than days and with no block on the page updated since; columns updated-at (ms) and page. To check the blocks it reads every block's page once when two or more stale pages remain, so on a graph with more than about 20,000 blocks it can answer 422 (the scan cap): a larger days or a narrower tag leaves fewer pages to check."
+                         " meetings-without-decision (params meeting_tag, default Business Meeting, decision_tag, default Decision, and days, default 90): pages or blocks tagged meeting_tag created within days with no block below them, at any depth, tagged decision_tag; columns created-at (ms) and meeting. It walks up from each decision-tagged block, so it reads the decisions and their ancestors, not every block."
+                         " open-tasks-by-client (param client_property, default forClient): blocks tagged Task whose status is not Done or Canceled, with each client from the task's own client_property, else each from its page's, else empty, sorted by client title, so a task with two clients is a row under each; columns client and task. client_property must be a node, default (text), url or date property (its values have titles); a number, checkbox or datetime property is refused."
+                         " pages-missing-property (params tag and property, both required): pages tagged tag, or a tag extending it, with no value for property; column page."
+                         " Every default can be overridden in params, e.g. {\"tag\": \"Client\", \"days\": 14}. A tag param is a tag title (exact, else ignoring case), UUID or ident, and covers every tag extending it; a property param is a property title, UUID or ident; days is an integer from 1 to 3650. Rows come back sorted by the first column (oldest first for the dated queries, by client title for tasks, in creation order for pages-missing-property), each page or task pulled as uuid and title, so the uuid is a handle for the other tools."
+                         " The response is datalog_query's (columns, rows, total-rows, returned, truncated) plus name, the resolved params (titles and idents; a tag's includes lists the tags extending it) and the query, inputs and rules as EDN, which run as the same query through datalog_query."
+                         " The query runs under every datalog_query guard: a read-only view, the private filter (anything tagged Private, on a private page or under a private block is left out, and so is anything in the Recycle bin), the scan cap (20,000 datoms, 422), the join cap, the row cap and limit (1 to 200, default 50) and the byte cap. A param that doesn't resolve or is out of range answers 400 naming it (params.<name>), never empty rows, including a default the graph doesn't have (no Business Meeting tag, say): pass the graph's own tag or property instead. An unknown name answers 404 listing the names. With dry-run=true the resolved params and the query, inputs and rules come back without running. Unavailable for E2EE graphs (409): titles are ciphertext.")]
    "getE2eePublicKeys" ["List graph members' public keys" "Returns the RSA-OAEP public keys of every member of an E2EE graph for wrapping the shared graph AES key. Only available on E2EE-encrypted graphs."]
    "encryptE2eeText" ["Encrypt text for an E2EE graph" "Encrypts each supplied string into the canonical Transit [iv ciphertext] package accepted by E2EE title and name fields. Requires 'key': the base64 raw 32-byte graph AES key, obtained from GET /e2ee/graphs/:id/aes-key and unwrapped with the user's RSA private key. Encrypt normalized, lowercase names separately from display titles."]
    "decryptE2eeText" ["Decrypt text from an E2EE graph" "Decrypts each supplied canonical encrypted package back to plaintext. Requires 'key': the base64 raw 32-byte AES-GCM-256 graph key. Only available on E2EE-encrypted graphs."]})
